# Recommendation: Amazon ElastiCache for Valkey

**Why this choice:**
- **Valkey** is the open-source, Linux Foundation fork of Redis. It's wire-compatible, so standard Redis clients and session libraries (`connect-redis`, `django-redis`, Spring Session, etc.) work unchanged. On ElastiCache it's about **20% cheaper than Redis OSS for node-based clusters** and about **33% cheaper for serverless**.
- **Not Memcached.** Sessions need replication and failover. Memcached has neither, so a node loss logs users out.
- **Not MemoryDB.** It offers durable, multi-AZ transaction-log storage. That's overkill and costs more for sessions and cache. Only consider it if losing sessions would be a real business problem.
- **Node-based, cluster mode disabled, 1 primary + 1 replica across AZs.** This gives predictable cost, automatic failover, and the simplest client config (a single endpoint, no cluster-aware client needed). You can move to cluster mode later if you outgrow one node's memory.

## Terraform

```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.5"
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = ">= 5.80" # Valkey engine support
    }
    random = {
      source  = "hashicorp/random"
      version = ">= 3.5"
    }
  }
}

# ---------- Inputs ----------

variable "name" {
  type    = string
  default = "webapp-cache"
}

variable "vpc_id" {
  type = string
}

variable "private_subnet_ids" {
  description = "Private subnets in at least 2 AZs"
  type        = list(string)
}

variable "app_security_group_id" {
  description = "Security group of the app tier (ECS tasks, EC2, Lambda, etc.)"
  type        = string
}

variable "node_type" {
  type    = string
  default = "cache.t4g.small" # move to cache.r7g.large+ as traffic grows
}

variable "replica_count" {
  type    = number
  default = 1
}

# ---------- Networking ----------

resource "aws_elasticache_subnet_group" "this" {
  name       = "${var.name}-subnets"
  subnet_ids = var.private_subnet_ids
}

resource "aws_security_group" "cache" {
  name        = "${var.name}-sg"
  description = "ElastiCache Valkey access from app tier only"
  vpc_id      = var.vpc_id
}

resource "aws_vpc_security_group_ingress_rule" "from_app" {
  security_group_id            = aws_security_group.cache.id
  referenced_security_group_id = var.app_security_group_id
  ip_protocol                  = "tcp"
  from_port                    = 6379
  to_port                      = 6379
  description                  = "Valkey from app"
}

# ---------- Auth ----------

resource "random_password" "auth" {
  length           = 64
  special          = true
  override_special = "!&#$^<>-" # ElastiCache disallows / " @ and spaces
}

resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret" "cache_auth" {
  name = "${var.name}/auth-token"
}

resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret_version" "cache_auth" {
  secret_id = aws_secretsmanager_secret.cache_auth.id
  secret_string = jsonencode({
    host  = aws_elasticache_replication_group.this.primary_endpoint_address
    port  = 6379
    token = random_password.auth.result
    tls   = true
  })
}

# ---------- Parameters ----------

resource "aws_elasticache_parameter_group" "this" {
  name   = "${var.name}-valkey8"
  family = "valkey8"

  # Evict only keys that have a TTL. Set TTLs on sessions AND cache entries.
  # Avoid allkeys-lru here: under memory pressure it can evict active sessions.
  parameter {
    name  = "maxmemory-policy"
    value = "volatile-lru"
  }
}

# ---------- Logs ----------

resource "aws_cloudwatch_log_group" "slow" {
  name              = "/elasticache/${var.name}/slow-log"
  retention_in_days = 14
}

# ---------- Cluster ----------

resource "aws_elasticache_replication_group" "this" {
  replication_group_id = var.name
  description          = "Sessions and cache for web app"

  engine               = "valkey"
  engine_version       = "8.0"
  node_type            = var.node_type
  port                 = 6379
  parameter_group_name = aws_elasticache_parameter_group.this.name

  num_cache_clusters         = 1 + var.replica_count # primary + replicas
  automatic_failover_enabled = true
  multi_az_enabled           = true

  subnet_group_name  = aws_elasticache_subnet_group.this.name
  security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.cache.id]

  at_rest_encryption_enabled = true
  transit_encryption_enabled = true
  auth_token                 = random_password.auth.result

  snapshot_retention_limit = 3
  snapshot_window          = "03:00-04:00"
  maintenance_window       = "sun:05:00-sun:06:00"
  auto_minor_version_upgrade = true
  apply_immediately          = false

  log_delivery_configuration {
    destination      = aws_cloudwatch_log_group.slow.name
    destination_type = "cloudwatch-logs"
    log_format       = "json"
    log_type         = "slow-log"
  }

  lifecycle {
    ignore_changes = [auth_token] # rotate deliberately, not on every plan
  }
}

# ---------- Outputs ----------

output "primary_endpoint" {
  value = aws_elasticache_replication_group.this.primary_endpoint_address
}

output "reader_endpoint" {
  value = aws_elasticache_replication_group.this.reader_endpoint_address
}

output "auth_secret_arn" {
  value = aws_secretsmanager_secret.cache_auth.arn
}

output "security_group_id" {
  value = aws_security_group.cache.id
}
```

## Things to know

1. **Connect over TLS.** Use `rediss://:<token>@<primary_endpoint>:6379`. Note the double "s". Plaintext connections will fail.
2. **Point writes at the primary endpoint.** It follows failover automatically. Use the reader endpoint only for read-heavy cache lookups that can tolerate slight replica lag. Sessions should always go to the primary.
3. **Set TTLs on everything.** With `volatile-lru`, keys without a TTL are never evicted, and the node can hit OOM errors. If you'd rather isolate the workloads, run two replication groups: one for sessions (`noeviction` or `volatile-lru`) and one for cache (`allkeys-lru`).
4. **Grant the app read access to the secret.** Its IAM role needs `secretsmanager:GetSecretValue` on `auth_secret_arn`.
5. **Watch these CloudWatch metrics:** `DatabaseMemoryUsagePercentage`, `Evictions`, `EngineCPUUtilization`, `CurrConnections`. Upsize the node type when memory stays above ~70%.
6. **Terraform state contains the auth token.** Make sure your state backend (e.g., an S3 bucket) is encrypted and access-restricted.

## Alternative: serverless

If you'd rather not size nodes at all, ElastiCache Serverless for Valkey has a low minimum (100 MB metered) and scales automatically:

```hcl
resource "aws_elasticache_serverless_cache" "this" {
  name                 = var.name
  engine               = "valkey"
  major_engine_version = "8"
  subnet_ids           = var.private_subnet_ids
  security_group_ids   = [aws_security_group.cache.id]

  cache_usage_limits {
    data_storage {
      maximum = 5
      unit    = "GB"
    }
    ecpu_per_second {
      maximum = 5000
    }
  }

  snapshot_retention_limit = 3
}
```

It's a good fit for spiky or unknown traffic at launch. The node-based setup above is usually cheaper once traffic is steady and predictable. Serverless uses IAM or user-group (RBAC) auth rather than a single auth token, so adjust the secret handling accordingly.

# Recommendation: Amazon ElastiCache for Valkey

**Why ElastiCache for Valkey:**
- **Valkey** is the open-source (BSD) fork of Redis, maintained by the Linux Foundation and backed by AWS. It's wire-compatible with Redis, so standard Redis clients and session libraries (`connect-redis`, Spring Session, `django-redis`, etc.) work unchanged.
- It's **cheaper than ElastiCache for Redis OSS** on AWS (roughly 20% less for node-based, 33% less for serverless).
- It handles both of your use cases: TTL-based sessions and general caching.

**Why not the alternatives:**
- **Memcached**: no replication or persistence. A node failure logs everyone out.
- **MemoryDB**: durable and Multi-AZ transactional, but it costs more and is overkill unless the store is your primary database.
- **DynamoDB + DAX**: possible for sessions, but more complex and expensive for plain caching.

**Configuration below:** a node-based replication group with a primary and one replica across AZs, automatic failover, encryption in transit and at rest, an AUTH token stored in Secrets Manager, and slow logs sent to CloudWatch. Node-based pricing is more predictable than Serverless once you have steady traffic. A Serverless variant is at the end if you'd rather not size nodes.

---

## Terraform

### `versions.tf`
```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.5"
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = ">= 5.80"
    }
    random = {
      source  = "hashicorp/random"
      version = ">= 3.5"
    }
  }
}
```

### `variables.tf`
```hcl
variable "name" {
  description = "Name prefix for resources"
  type        = string
  default     = "webapp-cache"
}

variable "environment" {
  type    = string
  default = "prod"
}

variable "vpc_id" {
  type = string
}

variable "private_subnet_ids" {
  description = "Private subnets in at least 2 AZs"
  type        = list(string)
}

variable "app_security_group_ids" {
  description = "Security groups of the app tier (ECS tasks, EC2, Lambda) allowed to connect"
  type        = list(string)
}

variable "node_type" {
  description = "Start small; move to cache.r7g.* for memory-heavy prod workloads"
  type        = string
  default     = "cache.t4g.medium"
}

variable "replica_count" {
  description = "Number of read replicas (>=1 required for Multi-AZ failover)"
  type        = number
  default     = 1
}
```

### `main.tf`
```hcl
locals {
  tags = {
    Name        = var.name
    Environment = var.environment
    ManagedBy   = "terraform"
  }
}

# --- Networking -------------------------------------------------------------

resource "aws_elasticache_subnet_group" "this" {
  name       = "${var.name}-subnets"
  subnet_ids = var.private_subnet_ids
  tags       = local.tags
}

resource "aws_security_group" "cache" {
  name        = "${var.name}-sg"
  description = "ElastiCache Valkey access from app tier"
  vpc_id      = var.vpc_id
  tags        = local.tags
}

resource "aws_vpc_security_group_ingress_rule" "from_app" {
  for_each = toset(var.app_security_group_ids)

  security_group_id            = aws_security_group.cache.id
  referenced_security_group_id = each.value
  ip_protocol                  = "tcp"
  from_port                    = 6379
  to_port                      = 6379
  description                  = "Valkey from app"
}

# No egress rule needed; ElastiCache doesn't initiate outbound connections.

# --- Auth token -------------------------------------------------------------

resource "random_password" "auth" {
  length  = 64
  special = false # ElastiCache restricts allowed special characters; alnum is safest
}

resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret" "auth" {
  name        = "${var.name}/auth-token"
  description = "AUTH token for ${var.name} ElastiCache"
  tags        = local.tags
}

resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret_version" "auth" {
  secret_id = aws_secretsmanager_secret.auth.id
  secret_string = jsonencode({
    auth_token       = random_password.auth.result
    primary_endpoint = aws_elasticache_replication_group.this.primary_endpoint_address
    reader_endpoint  = aws_elasticache_replication_group.this.reader_endpoint_address
    port             = 6379
    tls              = true
  })
}

# --- Parameters -------------------------------------------------------------

resource "aws_elasticache_parameter_group" "this" {
  name   = "${var.name}-valkey8"
  family = "valkey8"

  # Only evict keys that have a TTL. Sessions and cache entries should both
  # have TTLs. Anything without one (e.g. config) is never evicted.
  parameter {
    name  = "maxmemory-policy"
    value = "volatile-lru"
  }

  tags = local.tags
}

# --- Logs -------------------------------------------------------------------

resource "aws_cloudwatch_log_group" "slow" {
  name              = "/elasticache/${var.name}/slow-log"
  retention_in_days = 14
  tags              = local.tags
}

# --- Cluster ----------------------------------------------------------------

resource "aws_elasticache_replication_group" "this" {
  replication_group_id = var.name
  description          = "Sessions + cache for ${var.name}"

  engine               = "valkey"
  engine_version       = "8.0"
  node_type            = var.node_type
  port                 = 6379
  parameter_group_name = aws_elasticache_parameter_group.this.name

  # Cluster mode disabled: one shard, primary + replicas.
  # Simplest for session libs; scale vertically or enable cluster mode later.
  num_cache_clusters         = 1 + var.replica_count
  automatic_failover_enabled = var.replica_count > 0
  multi_az_enabled           = var.replica_count > 0

  subnet_group_name  = aws_elasticache_subnet_group.this.name
  security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.cache.id]

  # Security
  at_rest_encryption_enabled = true
  transit_encryption_enabled = true
  auth_token                 = random_password.auth.result
  auth_token_update_strategy = "ROTATE"

  # Maintenance & backups
  maintenance_window         = "sun:05:00-sun:06:00"
  snapshot_window            = "03:00-04:00"
  snapshot_retention_limit   = 3
  auto_minor_version_upgrade = true
  apply_immediately          = false

  log_delivery_configuration {
    destination      = aws_cloudwatch_log_group.slow.name
    destination_type = "cloudwatch-logs"
    log_format       = "json"
    log_type         = "slow-log"
  }

  tags = local.tags

  lifecycle {
    ignore_changes = [num_cache_clusters] # if you scale replicas out-of-band
  }
}

# --- Alarms (minimal) -------------------------------------------------------

resource "aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm" "memory" {
  alarm_name          = "${var.name}-memory-high"
  namespace           = "AWS/ElastiCache"
  metric_name         = "DatabaseMemoryUsagePercentage"
  statistic           = "Average"
  period              = 300
  evaluation_periods  = 2
  threshold           = 80
  comparison_operator = "GreaterThanThreshold"
  dimensions          = { ReplicationGroupId = aws_elasticache_replication_group.this.id }
  tags                = local.tags
  # alarm_actions = [aws_sns_topic.alerts.arn]
}

resource "aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm" "cpu" {
  alarm_name          = "${var.name}-engine-cpu-high"
  namespace           = "AWS/ElastiCache"
  metric_name         = "EngineCPUUtilization"
  statistic           = "Average"
  period              = 300
  evaluation_periods  = 2
  threshold           = 75
  comparison_operator = "GreaterThanThreshold"
  dimensions          = { ReplicationGroupId = aws_elasticache_replication_group.this.id }
  tags                = local.tags
}
```

### `outputs.tf`
```hcl
output "primary_endpoint" {
  value = aws_elasticache_replication_group.this.primary_endpoint_address
}

output "reader_endpoint" {
  value = aws_elasticache_replication_group.this.reader_endpoint_address
}

output "auth_secret_arn" {
  description = "Grant your app role secretsmanager:GetSecretValue on this"
  value       = aws_secretsmanager_secret.auth.arn
}

output "security_group_id" {
  value = aws_security_group.cache.id
}
```

---

## Things to get right in the app

1. **Use TLS.** Connect with `rediss://` (double "s") and pass the AUTH token. Plain `redis://` will fail because transit encryption is enforced.
2. **Set TTLs on everything.** With `volatile-lru`, keys without a TTL are never evicted, and a full cache will start rejecting writes. Set session TTL to your idle timeout and give cache entries their own TTLs.
3. **Namespace keys**, e.g. `sess:{id}` and `cache:{...}`. If cache churn ever threatens sessions, split them into two replication groups. That's a common step at scale.
4. **Route reads sensibly.** Session reads should go to the primary endpoint, since replicas can lag slightly. Tolerant cache reads can use the reader endpoint.
5. **Mind the Terraform state.** The `random_password` value ends up in Terraform state, so use an encrypted remote backend (S3 + KMS) with restricted access.

## Alternative: ElastiCache Serverless (no node sizing)

This is a good fit for spiky or unknown traffic. You pay per GB stored and per request, Multi-AZ is built in, and TLS is always on.

```hcl
resource "aws_elasticache_serverless_cache" "this" {
  name                 = var.name
  engine               = "valkey"
  major_engine_version = "8"
  subnet_ids           = var.private_subnet_ids
  security_group_ids   = [aws_security_group.cache.id]

  cache_usage_limits {
    data_storage {
      maximum = 10
      unit    = "GB"
    }
    ecpu_per_second {
      maximum = 5000
    }
  }

  snapshot_retention_limit = 3
  daily_snapshot_time      = "03:00"
  tags                     = local.tags
}
```

For Serverless, use IAM auth or an ElastiCache user group (RBAC) instead of an AUTH token.

**Rule of thumb:** start with Serverless if traffic is unpredictable or small. Switch to the node-based config above once usage is steady, since it gets noticeably cheaper at sustained load.

# Recommendation: Amazon ElastiCache for Valkey

**Why this choice:**
- **Valkey** is the open-source (BSD) fork of Redis, backed by AWS, Google, and the Linux Foundation. It works with existing Redis clients and session libraries (`connect-redis`, Spring Session, `django-redis`, etc.).
- On ElastiCache, Valkey is **about 20% cheaper per node** than Redis OSS, and about 33% cheaper on Serverless, with the same feature set.
- **Use a replication group with Multi-AZ and automatic failover.** Losing a cache node is fine. Logging out every user is not.

**When to pick something else:**
- **MemoryDB.** Choose it if sessions must survive a full-cluster failure, since it provides a durable transaction log. It costs more and has slower writes.
- **ElastiCache Serverless.** Choose it if your traffic is spiky or unknown and you don't want to size nodes. An example is at the bottom.
- **DynamoDB.** Choose it if you only need sessions, not caching, and want zero ops.

## One design point before the code

You're putting sessions and cache in the same store, so eviction matters. Under memory pressure, `allkeys-lru` can evict live sessions. The config below uses **`volatile-lru`**, which only evicts keys that have a TTL. That means you must **set a TTL on every cache key**. At scale, consider two separate clusters.

## Terraform

### `versions.tf`
```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.5"
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = ">= 5.80" # Valkey engine support
    }
    random = {
      source  = "hashicorp/random"
      version = ">= 3.5"
    }
  }
}
```

### `variables.tf`
```hcl
variable "name" {
  description = "Name prefix for resources"
  type        = string
  default     = "webapp"
}

variable "environment" {
  type    = string
  default = "prod"
}

variable "vpc_id" {
  type = string
}

variable "private_subnet_ids" {
  description = "Private subnets in at least 2 AZs"
  type        = list(string)
}

variable "app_security_group_ids" {
  description = "Security groups of the app tier allowed to connect"
  type        = list(string)
}

variable "node_type" {
  type    = string
  default = "cache.t4g.medium" # use cache.r7g.large+ for production load
}

variable "replica_count" {
  description = "Number of replicas in addition to the primary"
  type        = number
  default     = 1
}
```

### `main.tf`
```hcl
locals {
  id   = "${var.name}-${var.environment}"
  port = 6379
  tags = {
    Application = var.name
    Environment = var.environment
    ManagedBy   = "terraform"
  }
}

# ---------- Networking ----------

resource "aws_elasticache_subnet_group" "this" {
  name       = "${local.id}-cache"
  subnet_ids = var.private_subnet_ids
  tags       = local.tags
}

resource "aws_security_group" "cache" {
  name        = "${local.id}-cache"
  description = "ElastiCache Valkey access from app tier"
  vpc_id      = var.vpc_id
  tags        = merge(local.tags, { Name = "${local.id}-cache" })
}

resource "aws_vpc_security_group_ingress_rule" "from_app" {
  for_each = toset(var.app_security_group_ids)

  security_group_id            = aws_security_group.cache.id
  referenced_security_group_id = each.value
  from_port                    = local.port
  to_port                      = local.port
  ip_protocol                  = "tcp"
  description                  = "Valkey from app"
}

# No egress rule needed; ElastiCache doesn't initiate connections.

# ---------- Auth ----------

resource "random_password" "auth" {
  length  = 64
  special = false # avoids disallowed chars (@ / " and spaces)
}

resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret" "cache_auth" {
  name        = "${local.id}/cache/auth-token"
  description = "Auth token for ${local.id} Valkey"
  tags        = local.tags
}

resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret_version" "cache_auth" {
  secret_id = aws_secretsmanager_secret.cache_auth.id
  secret_string = jsonencode({
    host     = aws_elasticache_replication_group.this.primary_endpoint_address
    port     = local.port
    password = random_password.auth.result
    tls      = true
  })
}

# ---------- Parameters ----------

resource "aws_elasticache_parameter_group" "this" {
  name   = "${local.id}-valkey8"
  family = "valkey8"

  # Only evict keys that have a TTL, so sessions aren't dropped
  # before expired/expiring cache entries.
  parameter {
    name  = "maxmemory-policy"
    value = "volatile-lru"
  }

  tags = local.tags
}

# ---------- Logs ----------

resource "aws_cloudwatch_log_group" "slow" {
  name              = "/elasticache/${local.id}/slow-log"
  retention_in_days = 14
  tags              = local.tags
}

resource "aws_cloudwatch_log_group" "engine" {
  name              = "/elasticache/${local.id}/engine-log"
  retention_in_days = 14
  tags              = local.tags
}

# ---------- Cluster ----------

resource "aws_elasticache_replication_group" "this" {
  replication_group_id = local.id
  description          = "Sessions and cache for ${local.id}"

  engine               = "valkey"
  engine_version       = "8.0"
  node_type            = var.node_type
  port                 = local.port
  parameter_group_name = aws_elasticache_parameter_group.this.name

  # Cluster mode disabled: 1 primary + N replicas.
  # Simplest for session libraries; scale vertically or move to cluster mode later.
  num_cache_clusters         = 1 + var.replica_count
  automatic_failover_enabled = var.replica_count > 0
  multi_az_enabled           = var.replica_count > 0

  subnet_group_name  = aws_elasticache_subnet_group.this.name
  security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.cache.id]

  # Security
  at_rest_encryption_enabled = true
  transit_encryption_enabled = true
  auth_token                 = random_password.auth.result
  auth_token_update_strategy = "ROTATE"

  # Ops
  snapshot_retention_limit   = 3
  snapshot_window            = "03:00-04:00"
  maintenance_window         = "sun:05:00-sun:06:00"
  auto_minor_version_upgrade = true
  apply_immediately          = false

  log_delivery_configuration {
    destination      = aws_cloudwatch_log_group.slow.name
    destination_type = "cloudwatch-logs"
    log_format       = "json"
    log_type         = "slow-log"
  }

  log_delivery_configuration {
    destination      = aws_cloudwatch_log_group.engine.name
    destination_type = "cloudwatch-logs"
    log_format       = "json"
    log_type         = "engine-log"
  }

  tags = local.tags

  lifecycle {
    precondition {
      condition     = length(var.private_subnet_ids) >= 2
      error_message = "Provide subnets in at least two AZs for Multi-AZ."
    }
  }
}

# ---------- Alarms (minimal) ----------

resource "aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm" "memory" {
  alarm_name          = "${local.id}-cache-memory-high"
  namespace           = "AWS/ElastiCache"
  metric_name         = "DatabaseMemoryUsagePercentage"
  statistic           = "Average"
  period              = 300
  evaluation_periods  = 2
  threshold           = 80
  comparison_operator = "GreaterThanThreshold"
  dimensions = {
    ReplicationGroupId = aws_elasticache_replication_group.this.id
  }
  tags = local.tags
}

resource "aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm" "evictions" {
  alarm_name          = "${local.id}-cache-evictions"
  namespace           = "AWS/ElastiCache"
  metric_name         = "Evictions"
  statistic           = "Sum"
  period              = 300
  evaluation_periods  = 1
  threshold           = 0
  comparison_operator = "GreaterThanThreshold"
  dimensions = {
    ReplicationGroupId = aws_elasticache_replication_group.this.id
  }
  tags = local.tags
}
```
Add `alarm_actions = [aws_sns_topic.alerts.arn]` to both alarms once you have an SNS topic.

### `outputs.tf`
```hcl
output "primary_endpoint" {
  value = aws_elasticache_replication_group.this.primary_endpoint_address
}

output "reader_endpoint" {
  value = aws_elasticache_replication_group.this.reader_endpoint_address
}

output "port" {
  value = local.port
}

output "auth_secret_arn" {
  description = "Grant your app's IAM role secretsmanager:GetSecretValue on this"
  value       = aws_secretsmanager_secret.cache_auth.arn
}

output "security_group_id" {
  value = aws_security_group.cache.id
}
```

## Gotchas
1. **TLS is required.** Clients must connect with `rediss://` or `tls: true`. This is the most common "can't connect" issue.
2. **Point session writes at the primary endpoint.** You can send cache reads to the reader endpoint, but replicas lag slightly.
3. **The auth token ends up in Terraform state.** Use an encrypted remote backend (S3 with KMS) and restrict access to it.
4. **Sizing.** Start with `cache.t4g.medium` for dev or staging. For production, use `r7g` nodes and watch the `DatabaseMemoryUsagePercentage` and `EngineCPUUtilization` metrics.

## Serverless alternative
If you'd rather not size nodes, swap the replication group for:
```hcl
resource "aws_elasticache_serverless_cache" "this" {
  name                 = local.id
  engine               = "valkey"
  major_engine_version = "8"
  subnet_ids           = var.private_subnet_ids
  security_group_ids   = [aws_security_group.cache.id]

  cache_usage_limits {
    data_storage {
      maximum = 10
      unit    = "GB"
    }
    ecpu_per_second {
      maximum = 5000
    }
  }

  snapshot_retention_limit = 3
  tags                     = local.tags
}
```
Serverless is always Multi-AZ, encrypted, and TLS-only. It doesn't use an auth token, so set up authentication with ElastiCache users and user groups (RBAC) instead. Serverless pays off for unpredictable traffic. At steady, high throughput, provisioned nodes are usually cheaper.

## Pick: Amazon ElastiCache for Valkey, Multi-AZ replication group

**Why this choice:**
- **Valkey** is the open-source, Redis-compatible fork that AWS now backs. It works with existing Redis clients and session libraries (`connect-redis`, `django-redis`, Spring Session, etc.) and costs about 20% less than ElastiCache for Redis OSS on node-based clusters.
- **Redis/Valkey over Memcached:** sessions need replication and failover. With Memcached, a node loss logs users out. You also get TTLs, data structures, and pub/sub.
- **Node-based over Serverless:** costs are predictable, and the floor is lower for a launch-sized workload. ElastiCache Serverless is a good alternative if your traffic is spiky or unknown and you'd rather not size nodes at all. Its minimum bill is higher, though. A variant is at the bottom.
- **Multi-AZ with automatic failover** protects sessions from an AZ outage.
- **TLS, at-rest encryption, and an AUTH token** stored in Secrets Manager.

