Notes:
- Store `redis_auth_token` in Secrets Manager/SSM and inject it into your app. Terraform state will contain it, so use encrypted remote state.
- Your app must connect with TLS and AUTH: `rediss://:<token>@<primary_endpoint>:6379`.
- Set TTLs on session/cache keys. For cache-only data, use an eviction policy like `allkeys-lru`.
- If your AWS provider doesn’t support Valkey yet, change `engine = "redis"` and `engine_version = "7.1"`.

**Pick: Amazon ElastiCache for Redis** (Valkey works identically), set up as a single-shard replication group with a replica in another AZ, automatic failover, TLS, and AUTH. Redis beats Memcached here because sessions benefit from persistence, Multi-AZ failover, richer data structures (hashes for session objects), and per-key TTLs — Memcached is fine for pure caching but has no failover or durability story.

Assumes you have an existing VPC with private subnets in ≥2 AZs and a security group for your app tier.

