Key things to know:

- **TLS + AUTH:** With transit encryption on, clients connect via `rediss://` using the auth token as the password. Most modern Redis clients handle this natively.
- **Valkey swap:** Set `engine = "valkey"`, `engine_version = "7.2"`. Same API/protocol, slightly cheaper per node than Redis OSS since the licensing change — worth considering for a new build.
- **TTLs:** Your app should write sessions with `SET key value EX 1800` (and refresh on activity) so they expire. The `allkeys-lru` policy then handles cache eviction. If you want sessions never evicted while cache is, use `volatile-lru` and ensure every cache key has a TTL.
- **Cluster mode:** Deliberately off. Enable it (`num_node_groups > 1`) only when you exceed ~250 GB per shard or need much higher write throughput — it complicates clients (hash tags, no cross-slot multi-key ops).
- **Sizing signal:** Watch CloudWatch `CPUUtilization`, `CacheHitRate`, and evictions. Any evictions = memory pressure → bump node type. `r7g` (Graviton) is the price/performance sweet spot.
- **Serverless option:** ElastiCache Serverless exists and is good for spiky/low-volume workloads, but node-based clusters are meaningfully cheaper at steady-state traffic like session serving.

**Pick: ElastiCache for Valkey** (AWS's managed Redis-compatible engine — drop-in for Redis clients, no license baggage). It's the standard answer for sessions + caching: sub-ms latency, native TTLs per key, replication with automatic failover, and every web framework has a Redis session/cache backend. Memcached doesn't give you replication, failover, or richer data structures; MemoryDB is overkill (durable/transactional).

Here's a production-ready setup — private subnets, multi-AZ with one replica, TLS + auth token, and an eviction policy that won't randomly log users out:

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws    = { source = "hashicorp/aws", version = "~> 5.0" }
    random = { source = "hashicorp/random", version = "~> 3.0" }
  }
}

variable "project"              { type = string }
variable "vpc_id"               { type = string }
variable "private_subnet_ids"   { type = list(string) } # ≥2 subnets, different AZs
variable "app_security_group_id" { type = string }      # your app tier's SG

# ElastiCache auth tokens reject most symbols, so keep it alphanumeric
resource "random_password" "cache_auth" {
  length  = 64
  special = false
}

resource "aws_security_group" "cache" {
  name_prefix = "${var.project}-cache-"
  vpc_id      = var.vpc_id

  ingress {
    description     = "Redis from app tier only"
    from_port       = 6379
    to_port         = 6379
    protocol        = "tcp"
    security_groups = [var.app_security_group_id]
  }

  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }
}

resource "aws_elasticache_subnet_group" "main" {
  name       = "${var.project}-cache-subnets"
  subnet_ids = var.private_subnet_ids
}

# volatile-lru: only evicts keys that have a TTL. Set a TTL on everything
# (cache keys = freshness window, session keys = session lifetime) and you
# get LRU caching behavior without sessions being evicted before expiry.
resource "aws_elasticache_parameter_group" "main" {
  family = "valkey7" # use "redis7" if engine = "redis"
  name   = "${var.project}-cache-params"

  parameter {
    name  = "maxmemory-policy"
    value = "volatile-lru"
  }
}

resource "aws_elasticache_replication_group" "main" {
  replication_group_id = "${var.project}-cache"
  description          = "User sessions and cache"

  engine         = "valkey"     # swap to "redis" (version "7.1") if preferred
  engine_version = "7.2"
  node_type      = "cache.r7g.large" # ~13 GiB; Graviton, best price/perf
  port           = 6379

  parameter_group_name = aws_elasticache_parameter_group.main.name
  subnet_group_name    = aws_elasticache_subnet_group.main.name
  security_group_ids   = [aws_security_group.cache.id]

  # Single shard + 1 cross-AZ replica. Cluster mode stays off — simpler
  # client config; only enable it if you outgrow one node (~tens of GB).
  num_node_groups         = 1
  replicas_per_node_group = 1
  automatic_failover_enabled = true
  multi_az_enabled           = true

  at_rest_encryption_enabled = true
  transit_encryption_enabled = true   # required when using auth_token
  auth_token                 = random_password.cache_auth.result

  snapshot_retention_limit = 5
}

output "endpoint" {
  value = aws_elasticache_replication_group.main.primary_endpoint_address
}

output "port" {
  value = 6379
}

output "auth_token" {
  value     = random_password.cache_auth.result
  sensitive = true
}
```

**Connecting from your app:**
- URL format: `rediss://:<auth_token>@<endpoint>:6379` — note **`rediss`** (TLS). Since transit encryption is on, plain `redis://` will fail. In ioredis: `tls = {}`; in redis-py: `ssl=True`; in go-redis: `EnableTLS`.
- Restrict the security group to your app tier's SG (the variable above does this) — never open 6379 to the VPC CIDR broadly.

**Things worth knowing:**
- **Set a TTL on every key.** This is the one discipline that makes the single-instance sessions+cache combo work safely with `volatile-lru`.
- **Failover is async replication**, so worst case you lose the last ~second of writes — a few users re-authenticate. Cache is regenerable anyway. If you need zero-write-loss, that's MemoryDB territory.
- **Scaling:** resizing `node_type` is online (brief failover during the swap). Vertical first, cluster mode later only if you genuinely need sharding.
- **ElastiCache Serverless** (`aws_elasticache_serverless_cache`) is the pay-per-use alternative if you can't predict load — but sustained throughput costs more than fixed nodes, so I'd start node-based for a typical launch and revisit if traffic turns out spiky.

If you tell me your framework (Express, Django, Rails, Spring…) I can include the exact session store + cache client config to wire this up.

**Pick: Amazon ElastiCache for Redis/Valkey.** Specifically a cluster-mode-disabled replication group with a primary + one replica across AZs, TLS + auth token, in private subnets.

Why this over Memcached: Redis/Valkey gives you replication with automatic Multi-AZ failover (losing your session store = everyone gets logged out), richer data types (hashes, sets, TTLs per key), persistence if you want it, and native TLS. Memcached's only real advantage is simple multi-threaded scaling, which doesn't matter at session-store scale.

One naming note: AWS won't offer Redis OSS versions past 7.1, so for a new launch use the **Valkey** engine (Redis-protocol compatible, drop-in client support). If you specifically need "Redis," flip `engine` to `"redis"` and version to `"7.1"`.

```hcl
terraform {
