### Wiring it up

- Your app reads the secret at boot and connects with the `rediss://` URL (note the double **s** — TLS is mandatory since `transit_encryption_enabled = true`). `node-redis`/`ioredis`, `go-redis`, `lettuce`/`jedis` all handle it natively.
- Session stores plug straight in: `connect-redis` + `express-session` (Node), Spring Session (Java), `django-redis` (Python), etc. They set TTLs for you.

### Notes

- **Valkey**: for a brand-new deployment, consider `engine = "valkey"` — same protocol, drop-in for all Redis clients, no license baggage, and typically ~20% cheaper. Just change the parameter group family to match. Everything above works identically.
- **Serverless**: if your traffic is wildly spiky/unpredictable, `aws_elasticache_serverless_cache` is an option, but its per-GB/per-ECPU pricing has a floor that makes it pricier than two t4g nodes (~$100/month) at launch scale.
- **Scaling path**: grow vertically first (`cache.m7g.large` etc.) — a single shard saturates far later than people expect. Only enable cluster mode when one node genuinely can't hold the keyspace.
- **MemoryDB** is the durable Redis variant for primary-database duty — overkill (and pricier) for sessions/cache; skip it.

## Recommendation: **Amazon ElastiCache for Redis**

For session storage + caching on AWS, Redis is the right default:

| Option | Verdict |
|---|---|
| **ElastiCache for Redis** | ✅ Pick this — sub-ms latency, TTL per key (sessions expire naturally), data structures (hashes for session blobs), replication + failover, encryption in transit/at rest |
| ElastiCache for Memcached | ❌ No replication, no failover, no TTL-ordered eviction guarantees, fewer client features. Only worth it for pure throwaway cache with multithreaded reads |
| DynamoDB | ❌ Durable/persistent — wrong tradeoff for an ephemeral session store, and you pay per-request for data you'll throw away anyway |
| Aurora | ❌ Disk-backed; latency and cost profile don't fit |

**Assumptions I'm making:** existing VPC with private subnets, an existing app security group, Redis 7.x, small footprint to start (1 shard × 1 replica), cluster mode enabled so you can scale shards later without migration. Tell me if any of those are wrong and I'll adjust.

