## The pick: **Amazon ElastiCache** — Valkey engine (or Redis OSS 7.x), two replication groups in private subnets

**Why this over the alternatives:**

| Option | Verdict |
|---|---|
| **ElastiCache (Valkey/Redis)** | Sub‑ms latency, AWS patches/backups/fails over for you, native TTL + LRU eviction, standard Redis protocol so every client library works. **This is the pick.** |
| ElastiCache **Serverless** | Legit alternative if you don't want to size nodes — you trade knobs (node type, some parameter tuning) for zero capacity planning. |
| **DynamoDB** | Great store, wrong shape for a hot LRU cache: no eviction under memory pressure, per‑request pricing on a hot read path. |
| **MemoryDB** | Priced for a *durable* database; sessions and cache are both ephemeral. Overkill. |
| Self‑managed Redis on EC2 | You now own patching, failover, backup, and 3am pages. No. |

**Two things that actually matter in the design:**

1. **Two clusters, not one.** Eviction policy is the fault line. Sessions want `volatile-lru` — evict a cold session (user re‑logs in) rather than fail a write with `OOM command not allowed`. Cache wants `allkeys-lru` — evict anything, a miss is free. Share one cluster and you either evict live sessions under memory pressure or OOM your cache writes. Also: **every session key must have a TTL**, or `volatile-*` has nothing to evict.
2. **Cluster mode disabled, 1 primary + 1 replica, Multi‑AZ + automatic failover.** One endpoint, no client‑side sharding, enough headroom for a launch. You switch to cluster mode later by swapping `num_cache_clusters` for `num_node_groups`/`replicas_per_node_group`.

Security posture: nodes in **private subnets only**, ingress **only from the app security groups**, encryption at rest **and** in transit, a **separate AUTH token per cluster** stored in SSM SecureString (never in git or in the image).

